RK3588S Orange Pi 5B HDMI Fixed Rate Link support.

Upstream drives the RK3588 HDMI TX over TMDS only, which caps the link at a
600 MHz character rate. Everything past that is refused before it reaches the
driver, so 4K60 at 10 bpc (742.5 MHz) and 4K120 in any full-chroma format are
simply absent from the mode list.

Rockchip's own kernel runs this exact board at 4K120 4:4:4 over FRL, so the
ceiling is a software one. This adds the missing link:

  - drm_hdmi_state_helper: let a mode past the TMDS ceiling when both ends can
    carry it over FRL, and pick the slowest standard FRL rate that fits. The
    vendor driver instead always requests the sink maximum and then clamps the
    result to 40 Gbps; choosing by payload keeps link margin and still allows
    FRL6 for modes that genuinely need it, such as 4K120 at 10 bpc, which wants
    40.1 Gbps and so does not fit the vendor ceiling at all.
  - drm_bridge/drm_connector: carry the source side FRL capability.
  - dw-hdmi-qp: FRL op mode, HDMI 2.1 link training (LTS1..LTS3 and LTSP with
    the FRL_START handshake), and TxFFE stepping when the sink asks for more
    equalisation.
  - dw_hdmi_qp-rockchip: choose TMDS or FRL during atomic_check, program the
    PHY accordingly, and drive the RK3588 GRF HDMI21 bit.
  - phy-rockchip-samsung-hdptx: apply TxFFE on the FRL lane setup.

Ordering matters and is taken from the vendor driver: the controller enters FRL
op mode before the PHY is powered, and link training runs only after the mode
is fully programmed. Training against a half configured pipeline reaches "all
lanes trained" and then the sink asks to retrain forever, with no picture.

Bringing the link back up after the display has blanked needs more patience
than a first-time modeset, and that took several rounds to get right:

  - A sink leaving standby takes seconds to answer DDC at all. SCDC reads fail
    outright or report version 0 until it is awake, so training waits for it.
  - It then answers SCDC well before it will agree to be trained. One measured
    wake replied to LTS1 immediately and raised FLT_ready only five seconds
    after the PHY began sending, hence the eight second LTS2 wait.
  - DDC keeps glitching for a while after the first successful reply, so the
    SCDC transfers retry rather than failing a whole round over one EIO.
  - Training therefore runs from a worker, not from atomic_enable, which would
    otherwise stall the modeset for the whole wait. The worker retries a failed
    bring-up: without that, one transient failure left the display dark until
    something else forced a modeset, which is what made a blanked screen
    impossible to wake.

The worker also watches SCDC for FLT_update once the link is up, which is how a
sink asks to be retrained after losing lock. Nothing was watching that flag.

